<BaseLayout title="CV4 (Guanyuan) — Gate of Origin Point" description="Everything you need to self-treat CV4 (Guanyuan) — exact location, when to use it, a step-by-step routine, symptom-based point combinations, and expected results." breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Points", href: "/points/" }, { label: "CV4 (Guanyuan)", href: "/points/cv4-guanyuan-acupressure-point/" }]} datePublished="2026-08-19" dateModified="2026-08-19" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
    <h1 class="mb-4">CV4 (Guanyuan) — Gate of Origin</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">CV4, known as Guanyuan or the Gate of Origin, is one of the most important points on the Conception Vessel. Located on the lower abdomen, it is classically regarded as a reservoir of the body's vital energy and is used for urinary, reproductive, and constitutional support — frequent urination, menstrual irregularity, fatigue, and a general sense of running cold.</p>

    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">At a Glance</h2>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Meridian</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Conception Vessel (CV / Ren Mai)</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Body region</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Lower abdomen, on the midline about four of your own finger-widths below the navel</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Best for</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Frequent urination and night waking, menstrual irregularity, painful periods, fatigue, low immunity, lower-abdominal cold</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Difficulty</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Easy to reach lying down; needs light, patient, breath-matched pressure</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Hold time</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">1–2 minutes per session, in slow circles</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Frequency</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">1–3 times daily</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Pairs well with</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><a href="/points/st36-zusanli-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">ST36 (Zusanli)</a>, <a href="/points/sp6-sanyinjiao-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">SP6 (Sanyinjiao)</a>, <a href="/points/ki1-yongquan-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">KI1 (Yongquan)</a></td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Skip if</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Pregnancy (especially the first trimester), recent abdominal or pelvic surgery, or within 30 minutes of a full meal</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why CV4 Matters</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">CV4 is the front-mu point of the Small Intestine and sits on the Conception Vessel, the midline channel that runs down the front of the body. Its name, Gate of Origin, reflects its traditional role as the place where the body's deepest vital energy, or yuan qi, is said to reside. In traditional Chinese medicine this makes CV4 a foundational point for rebuilding energy after illness, fatigue, or depletion, and it is frequently used for the lower abdomen and pelvic region.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Because it sits low on the abdomen, CV4 is closely linked to the bladder, uterus, and lower intestines in the classical system. Practitioners commonly reach for it for frequent urination, menstrual discomfort, and a general sense of low vitality or feeling cold. In self-care it pairs well with warmth — a warm compress over the point is a classic way to deepen its effect — and with slow, deep breathing to help the body settle into a restful state.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Where Is This Point Located?</h2>
    <img src="/images/cv4-guanyuan-location.webp" alt="CV4 acupressure point location on the lower abdomen below the navel" class="w-full max-w-sm mx-auto rounded-xl shadow-sm" width="300" height="188" loading="lazy">
    <p class="mb-3">CV4 is on the midline of the lower abdomen, three cun below the center of the belly button. A practical way to measure is to place four of your own fingers horizontally below the navel; the point sits just below the bottom edge of the little finger. For most people this lands roughly halfway between the navel and the top of the pubic bone, in the soft tissue of the lower abdomen. Because finger-width measurement varies from person to person, use your own fingers and adjust to where you feel the point most clearly.</p>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3"><strong>Three quick confirmations you have the right spot:</strong> (1) it sits on the exact midline, not off to either side; (2) it is soft tissue, roughly halfway between the navel and the pubic bone; (3) light pressure may produce a mild sense of warmth, fullness, or "pull," but never a sharp pain.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">When to Use CV4 (and When Not)</h2>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-forest-200 bg-forest-50 dark:border-forest-900/40 dark:bg-forest-900/20 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Use it when</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• You wake at night to urinate or urinate more often than usual</li>
          <li>• Menstrual periods are irregular, or cramps are uncomfortable</li>
          <li>• You feel persistently tired or drained and want a daily energy-maintenance point</li>
          <li>• The lower abdomen feels cold, or you generally run cold</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-stone-50 dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800/50 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Hold off if</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• You are pregnant — especially in the first trimester — unless your provider says otherwise</li>
          <li>• You have had recent abdominal or pelvic surgery</li>
          <li>• The lower abdomen is painful to the touch or the pain is unexplained</li>
          <li>• You have just eaten a full meal (wait at least 30 minutes)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Use the <em>Hold off</em> column as a safety filter: CV4 is a restorative, maintenance point, not first aid — and persistent urinary or pelvic symptoms deserve a medical checkup.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What Are the Benefits?</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Urinary health:</strong> Traditionally used for frequent urination, nocturia (waking at night to urinate), and general urinary discomfort.</li>
      <li><strong>Reproductive health:</strong> Classically used for menstrual irregularity, painful periods, and premenstrual discomfort.</li>
      <li><strong>Fatigue and low energy:</strong> A key point for restoring vitality and supporting recovery from prolonged tiredness.</li>
      <li><strong>Immune support:</strong> Traditionally regarded as a point that strengthens the body's defenses and resilience.</li>
      <li><strong>Lower abdominal cold:</strong> Used when the lower abdomen feels cold or the person generally runs cold.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How Do You Use This Point?</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">This point works best when the whole body is relaxed and the abdomen is soft. Lie down, breathe deeply, and keep the pressure slow and patient rather than hard.</p>
    <h3 class="mb-2 text-lg font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Step-by-step technique</h3>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Lie on your back with your knees slightly bent and a cushion under them so the abdomen relaxes.</li>
      <li>Place your middle finger (or the heel of your palm) on CV4, four of your own finger-widths below the belly button on the midline.</li>
      <li>Apply a firm but comfortable pressure and knead in slow circles for 1–2 minutes.</li>
      <li>Match the movement to your breath — press gently on the exhale and ease off on the inhale.</li>
      <li>Finish with a warm compress or heat pack over the point and rest for a few minutes.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed"><strong>Common mistake to avoid:</strong> pressing hard into a full bladder, straight after a heavy meal, or with a tensed abdominal wall. CV4 responds to light, breath-matched pressure on a relaxed belly — forcing it only creates discomfort without adding benefit.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Combine CV4 with These Points</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">A lower-abdominal routine is more effective than pressing one point. Pick the row closest to your situation and work the points in the order shown, 1–2 minutes each, with a warm compress over CV4 at the end.</p>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
          <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-44">Goal / symptom</th><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Routine (in order)</th></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Fatigue / low energy</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">CV4 → <a href="/points/st36-zusanli-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">ST36</a> → <a href="/points/ki1-yongquan-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">KI1</a>, 1–2 min each, morning or before rest</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Menstrual cramps / irregular periods</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">CV4 → <a href="/points/sp6-sanyinjiao-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">SP6</a> → ST36, 1 min each, 1–2 times daily around the period</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Frequent urination / nocturia</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">CV4 → SP6 → KI1 in the evening, then a warm compress over CV4; see a doctor if symptoms persist</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Lower-abdominal cold / low immunity</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">CV4 + ST36 + SP6, 1–2 min each, once daily as a maintenance routine</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">For a fuller program, see the <a href="/body/abdomen-acupressure-points/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">abdomen acupressure points</a> body guide and our <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-sleep/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">acupressure for sleep</a> page for a grounding wind-down routine.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What to Expect</h2>
    <div class="space-y-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">First session (today)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">The point may feel slightly tender, and most people notice a looser, warmer sensation in the lower abdomen within a few minutes of light circular pressure. Do not expect a change in urinary or menstrual patterns after one session.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Days 3–7 (consistent daily practice)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">With 1–2 sessions per day, the abdomen usually feels warmer and more settled, and some people report sleeping a little more deeply and waking feeling less drained. This matches the pattern in studies of acupressure for fatigue and menstrual discomfort, where benefits appear after several days of regular use.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Weeks 2–4</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">For chronic fatigue or menstrual irregularity, keep CV4 as part of a routine that includes adequate sleep, nutrition, and movement. Acupressure is a complement, not a replacement — and if symptoms such as night waking, heavy periods, or fatigue have not improved after 2–3 weeks, that is a signal to see a clinician.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What Does the Research Say?</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">Evidence on acupressure for fatigue, menstrual, and urinary complaints has grown in recent years. Systematic reviews and randomized controlled trials have examined acupressure as an adjunct for these conditions, with results that are generally promising but limited in quality. Lower-abdominal and midline points such as CV4 are among the most commonly studied in these protocols, and acupressure at abdominal points is frequently included in research on menstrual discomfort and energy restoration.</p>
    <p class="mb-3">Researchers consistently note that acupressure trials are hard to blind, because the treatment is physical and participants can usually tell whether they are being treated. This makes it difficult to separate the specific effects of a particular point from the general benefits of touch, pressure, and relaxation. The overall picture — which we discuss in detail on our <a href="/research/does-acupressure-work/">does acupressure work?</a> review — is one of encouraging signals alongside a clear need for larger, higher-quality studies before firm conclusions can be drawn.</p>

    <EvidenceBox refs={["chen-wang-2014"]} note="The review above evaluated acupressure for pain, including menstrual and pelvic discomfort where CV4 is traditionally used. It is not a study of CV4 in isolation." />

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Are There Safety Concerns?</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Avoid strong abdominal pressure during pregnancy — CV4 is traditionally avoided or used only very gently then; consult a midwife or doctor first, especially in the first trimester.</li>
      <li>Do not press within 30 minutes after a full meal, or over a full bladder.</li>
      <li>Do not press if you have had recent abdominal or pelvic surgery.</li>
      <li>Skip the point if there is skin irritation, a rash, or a hernia near the lower abdomen.</li>
      <li>Stop if pressure causes sharp pain or cramping, and seek medical advice for unexplained abdominal pain.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Red Flags — See a Professional</h2>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-red-200 bg-red-50 dark:border-red-900/40 dark:bg-red-900/20 p-4 text-sm">
      <p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-2">Stop self-treatment and see a doctor if you notice any of the following:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
        <li>Unexplained or worsening pain in the lower abdomen or pelvis</li>
        <li>Blood in the urine, or painful or burning urination</li>
        <li>Missed periods with possible pregnancy, or unusually heavy or prolonged bleeding</li>
        <li>A hard or bulging mass felt in the lower abdomen</li>
        <li>Symptoms that have not improved after 2–3 weeks of regular acupressure</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How do I find CV4 on myself?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Place four of your own fingers horizontally below the belly button; CV4 sits just below the bottom edge of the little finger, on the exact midline. For most people this is roughly halfway between the navel and the pubic bone. Always measure with your own fingers, since hand size varies.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long and how often should I press CV4?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Knead the point in slow circles for 1–2 minutes at the deepest comfortable pressure, matched to your breath, and repeat 1–3 times daily. Increase pressure slowly and stop if you feel sharp pain or cramping. Consistency (daily) matters more than a single long session.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can CV4 help with frequent urination or night waking?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">CV4 is traditionally used for frequent urination and nocturia, and it may help as a supportive practice — a short session in the evening with a warm compress can be soothing before bed. But night waking or changes in urination can also signal conditions that need a medical checkup, so do not rely on acupressure alone if symptoms persist.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Is CV4 safe during pregnancy?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Abdominal points such as CV4 are traditionally avoided or only used very gently during pregnancy, especially in the first trimester. If you are pregnant, speak with your midwife or doctor before practicing acupressure on the lower abdomen.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">When should I see a doctor instead of using CV4?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">See a doctor for blood or pain with urination, unexplained abdominal or pelvic pain, a mass in the abdomen, missed periods with possible pregnancy, or heavy/abnormal bleeding. Acupressure is a supportive practice, not a substitute for a proper diagnosis when symptoms are new, severe, or persistent.</div>
    </details>
    </section>
    <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">Related guides: <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-menstrual-cramps/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">acupressure for menstrual cramps</a> and <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-fertility/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">fertility</a>.</p>

